
# 🌐 VLAN Department Segmentation Lab

![Cisco Packet Tracer](https://img.shields.io/badge/Cisco-Packet%20Tracer-049FD9?logo=cisco&logoColor=white)
![Networking](https://img.shields.io/badge/Networking-VLANs-blue)
![Level](https://img.shields.io/badge/Level-Beginner-green)

## 📌 Project Overview

This project demonstrates how to configure and organize Virtual Local Area Networks (VLANs) using Cisco Packet Tracer.

The network uses a Cisco 2960 switch, a router, and six PCs divided into three departments: Engineering, Human Resources (HR), and Sales. Each department has its own VLAN and IP subnet.

The purpose is to practice VLAN creation, access-port assignment, IP addressing, and network connectivity verification.

## 🎯 Objectives

- Understand the purpose of VLANs and network segmentation.
- Create VLAN 10, VLAN 20, and VLAN 30.
- Assign switch ports to the appropriate VLANs.
- Configure IP addresses and subnet masks on end devices.
- Verify VLAN configuration using Cisco IOS commands.
- Test network connectivity using ping.
- Understand the role of Layer 3 routing in inter-VLAN communication.

## 🖥️ Network Topology

![VLAN Lab Topology](screenshots/topology.png)

*Figure 1: Three departmental VLANs connected to a Cisco 2960 switch.*

## 🧰 Devices and Technologies

| Device / Technology | Quantity | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| Cisco 2960 switch | 1 | VLAN creation and Layer 2 switching |
| Router | 1 | Intended for routing between VLANs, if configured |
| PCs | 6 | End devices for testing |
| VLANs | 3 | Departmental network segmentation |
| Cisco Packet Tracer | 1 | Network simulation |

## 🏢 VLAN and IP Addressing Plan

| Department | VLAN ID | Network | Example Hosts |
|---|---:|---|---|
| Engineering | 10 | `10.0.0.0/26` | `10.0.0.1`, `10.0.0.2` |
| HR | 20 | `10.0.0.64/26` | `10.0.0.65`, `10.0.0.66` |
| Sales | 30 | `10.0.0.128/26` | `10.0.0.129`, `10.0.0.130` |

Each subnet uses a `/26` prefix (`255.255.255.192`), providing 62 usable IPv4 host addresses per subnet.

## ⚙️ Implementation Steps

1. Build the topology in Cisco Packet Tracer.
2. Connect the six PCs to the switch and connect the router as shown in the topology.
3. Create VLAN 10, VLAN 20, and VLAN 30.
4. Assign the appropriate switch access ports to each VLAN.
5. Configure each PC with its assigned IP address and subnet mask.
6. Verify VLAN membership using `show vlan brief`.
7. Test connectivity using ping and document the results.

## 💻 Useful Cisco IOS Commands

Create the departmental VLANs:

```text
enable
configure terminal

vlan 10
 name ENGINEERING
exit

vlan 20
 name HR
exit

vlan 30
 name SALES
exit
```

Example access-port configuration:

```text
interface fastEthernet 0/1
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 10
exit
```

Verify the VLANs and assigned ports:

```text
show vlan brief
```

*Note: Adjust interface names and port numbers to match your actual Packet Tracer topology.*

## 🧪 Testing and Verification

- Confirm that each PC has the correct IP address and subnet mask.
- Verify that each switch port belongs to the intended VLAN.
- Test communication between PCs in the same VLAN, if multiple PCs are available in that VLAN.
- Test communication between different VLANs.
- Record successful and unsuccessful ping results and investigate any unexpected behavior.

**Important:** Inter-VLAN communication requires a configured Layer 3 routing function, appropriate gateway addresses, and correct routing configuration. The presence of a router in the topology alone does not guarantee connectivity between VLANs.

